    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d744">

                <pb facs="0699" n="527" sameAs="frus:frus1917Supp01v01/pg527"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 341.622a/296</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the
                        Ambassador in Great Britain</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Page</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1917-02-28T10:00:00-05:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">February 28, 1917, 10 a.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>4485. Your 5761, February 26. Please telegraph me your views as to advisability
                    of presenting arbitration proposal now or waiting until after receipt of British
                    note. The point of Department’s contention <pb facs="0700" n="528"
                        xml:id="pg528"/> in its mail instruction No. 4520, December 1, is that
                    persons are not lawfully subject to seizure from American ships on the high seas
                    regardless of whether they are in the military service of an enemy of Great
                    Britain. Further discussion of the facts in each case, therefore, would seem to
                    be beside the question involved, which appears to be an entirely proper one for
                    arbitration.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Lansing</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
